It is tempting to imagine a refresh throwing the source away and reading it again. It does not. Every pass compares what the source offers now against what the base already holds:

- **new** — ingested and embedded;
- **changed** — read again *under the same document id*, so citations already sitting in someone's chat history still point at something;
- **gone** — removed from the base;
- **unchanged** — nothing at all happens, which is the case that has to be cheap.

That is why a nightly schedule on a 40-page site is not extravagant: on a quiet night it costs almost nothing. Each pass also has a time budget, and whatever it does not finish simply stays due for the next one.
